# Spec 0040 — Faceted tree UX, facet totality, chart applicability, and sample geometry / R–T

Four independent Optical Constructor improvements, grounded in the current
post-0038 tree and reusing existing types throughout. The authoritative source
is the operator's preliminary spec
`specs/0038/.manual/011-tree-chart-geometry-spec.md`; the binding operator
answers it cites (010/Q1–Q3) are carried into the directives below. The
ordered, testable work is the steps in `.spec-jsonl`; this document is the
steering narrative only.

## 0. Binding constraints (every part)

0.1 **Reuse, don't invent.** Extend the existing types — never add a parallel
concept. R/T is the existing `Emission` DU (`Placement.fs:108-138`); the tree
is the existing `FacetedTreeControls.TreeNode` recursive model
(`FacetedTreeControls.fs:102-109`) with its `NodeExpansion`
(`:46-48`); the facet engine is the existing `Facets.buildTree`
(`Facets.fs:230-254`) and the `LibraryFacets` catalogue
(`LibraryFacets.fs`); the gyration/μ component vocabularies are the existing
`MaterialComplexityEditor` seams (`gyrationComponents`
`MaterialComplexityEditor.fs:436-444`, `MuKind` `:72-74`). This spec adds NO
new file or module: every change edits an existing one.

0.2 **CLAUDE.md discipline.** Elevate every primitive (single-case DUs with
`.value`); no naked `bool` (named two-case DUs); no `enum`; explicit concrete
signatures on public functions; interpolated strings never `sprintf`;
four-space indent; a space before every annotation colon; camelCase record
fields; LF endings; **zero warnings from our code** (only `NU1701` /
`NU1901`–`NU1904` exempt); a green build is non-negotiable. `FacetedTreeControls`
is DOMAIN-FREE: its node `code`s are opaque host-supplied `string` tokens
(the elevation boundary is the host), so new control-local fields stay
`string`-shaped to match the module's own convention (`activeRepresentation`,
`FacetedTreeControls.fs:124`, `140`) — do NOT elevate inside the control.

0.3 **UI testable without a window.** Behaviour lives in pure Domain functions
and in the control-local `State` / `Handlers` records (`FacetedTreeControls.fs`),
unit-tested in `OpticalConstructor.Tests` and structure/interaction-tested in
`Avalonia.Headless` (`OpticalConstructor.Ui.Tests`, the `ui-smoke` gate).
New/changed interactive controls carry stable intent-named
`AutomationProperties.AutomationId` from the one `UiIds` module
(`OpticalConstructor.Controls/UiIds.fs`); generated list rows never set
`StyledElement.Name` and are keyed with `View.withKey` (the existing
`FacetedTreeControls.fs:364` idiom).

0.4 **Scope discipline.** This spec does NOT add caching, retries,
configuration knobs, versioning, migration code, or backwards-compatibility
shims. The material corpus is the authored **seed** data only — no library
STATE is persisted — so every facet-totality property (Part B) is a **seeding**
property, never a runtime reclassification: no facet MUST evaluate the assembled
engine ε/μ/ρ tensor to classify a material.

---

## Part A — The faceted tree: sorted, collapsible, collapsed by default, selection-visible

**A.0 Problem.** In both catalogue windows the tree renders every node
expanded, in non-alphabetical order, with the selected node visually
indistinct. `FacetedTreeControls.nodeRows` (`FacetedTreeControls.fs:351-368`)
already switches on `node.expansion`, but every projected node hardcodes
`ExpandedNode` (`MaterialsWindowView.fs:719-760`;
`LibraryWindowView.fs:844-898`), `Handlers` (`FacetedTreeControls.fs:149-167`)
carries `selectNode` but no toggle, every row paints the same `idleBackground`
(`FacetedTreeControls.fs:355`), and ordering is structural, not alphabetical.

**A.1 Alphabetical at every level (operator, 010/Q1).** Order case-insensitively
by DISPLAY LABEL at all three levels: the top-level facet groups (overriding the
representation order — the operator requires the groups themselves
alphabetised), every branch, and every entry leaf. The branch sort lives in
`Facets.buildTree`, which today sorts by the structural value
(`Facets.fs:248`); the facet-group and entry-leaf ordering lives in each host's
tree assembly (`MaterialsWindowView.fs:719-760`, `LibraryWindowView.fs:844-898`).

**A.2 Collapsible, collapsed by default, persisted (operator).** Render the
recursive `TreeNode` with a per-node disclosure affordance (a chevron) that
toggles expand/collapse; a node with children is expandable, a leaf is not.
Add `Handlers.toggleNode : string -> unit` beside `selectNode`; the chevron
dispatches `toggleNode`, the row label keeps dispatching `selectNode`. Each
window holds its expansion state in its Model as an elevated named type wrapping
the set of expanded node codes (never a naked `Set<string>` on the record
surface), with a `Msg` + `update` arm, threaded into the projected `TreeNode`
tree so a re-projection with flipped `expansion` recreates cleanly (rows are
already keyed by `code`, `FacetedTreeControls.fs:364`). Every top-level node
projects `CollapsedNode` until the user expands it.

**A.3 Visible selection (operator).** Carry the selected node's `code` into the
control (a new `State` field, `string`-shaped per §0.2) and paint the selected
row with the existing `chosenBackground` (`FacetedTreeControls.fs:173`, the
`clickBox` highlight precedent, `:191-208`) as a distinct tint plus a
non-hue cue (border weight or a marker), colourblind-safe. Both hosts supply the
selected node's code.

---

## Part B — Every single-valued material facet sums to the whole population

**B.0 Problem and mechanism.** Under a representation the Anisotropy and
Transparency facets do not sum to the material population: they read the entry's
`complexity` value tree (`LibraryFacets.anisotropyOf`
`LibraryFacets.fs:132-143`, `transparencyOf` `:147-150`) and gate themselves off
when it is absent (`materialAnisotropyDef` `:217-232`, `materialTransparencyDef`
`:252-267`), so the three coded presets that carry `complexity = None` —
Silicon (`MaterialLibrary.fs:376`), Langasite (`:384`), and the Vacuum spacer
(`:440`) — fall out, and every dispersive material is excluded from Transparency
outright. **The fix is in the SEEDS, not at runtime.** Every seeded material
MUST carry its single-valued class as data readable without the engine tensor.
The chosen mechanism is a seeded `MaterialComplexity` value tree (the existing
`complexity = Some … ; properties = ….toProperties` seed convention,
`MaterialLibrary.fs:391-464`), so the same `anisotropyOf` / `transparencyOf`
read the class directly. The Dispersion facet already reads `properties` and is
total (`materialDispersion`, `MaterialLibrary.fs:606-609`); it stays as is.

**B.1 Re-seed the three coded presets.** Give Silicon, Langasite, and the
Vacuum spacer a `complexity = Some` value tree encoding the correct anisotropy —
Silicon **Isotropic**, Langasite **Uniaxial** (retaining its optical activity),
Vacuum **Isotropic** + **Transparent** + constant — with `properties`
re-pointed to `complexity.toProperties` per the seed convention, reproducing
each preset's physics through the existing dispersion-model ladder exactly as
the sibling dispersive/anisotropic seeds do (`euvSiliconComplexity`
`MaterialLibrary.fs:456`, `uniaxialCrystalComplexity` `:424`,
`activeCrystalComplexity` `:464` are the reuse precedents). Do NOT add a runtime
classifier that evaluates the engine ε tensor.

**B.2 Make Anisotropy and Transparency total.** Drop the `appliesTo` gates on
`materialAnisotropyDef` (`LibraryFacets.fs:217-232`) and `materialTransparencyDef`
(`:252-267`) so every material appears. Anisotropy reads `anisotropyOf` for every
material (all now carry `complexity`). Transparency reads `transparencyOf` for a
constant material and yields **Absorbing** for every dispersive material
(operator, 010/Q2), so the branch counts sum to the population. The multi-valued
Dispersion-model, Film-material, and Film-thickness facets are genuinely
multi-valued by design and stay outside this totality rule.

---

## Part C — Material-editor chart applicability

**C.1 Gyration & μ charts draw only the applicable components (operator).**
`gyrationChart` (`NkDispersionChart.fs:110-130`) emits all six gyration
components and `muChart` (`:141-162`) all three diagonals plus a gyration
magnitude, regardless of class. Restrict each chart to the *independent*
components of the current class, named to match, reusing the editor seams —
`gyrationComponents` (`MaterialComplexityEditor.fs:436-444`) yields exactly the
class's admitted components in display order, `gyrationComponentLabel` (`:421-428`)
names them, and each `GyrationComponent` maps to its (i, j) tensor slot
(`:88-94`); `MuKind` (`:72-74`) decides whether μ exposes a single scalar
component or the gyromagnetic diagonal-plus-gyration set. The class is threaded
from the editor ladder (`m.editor.gyration`, the magnetic `MuKind`) for an
`EditableMaterial`, and for a VIEW-ONLY entry from its stored `complexity` value
tree — mirroring `anisotropyOfEntry` (`NkDispersionChart.fs:74-77`); a coded
preset with no stored tree keeps the current all-components fallback (coincident
or zero components simply draw flat, exactly as the n/k chart already falls back
to Biaxial). The call sites are `MaterialEditorView.fs:1028-1046`.

**C.2 Description text box wrap (minor).** The material-editor description
`TextBox` has a fixed width and clips when the splitter is dragged left. Make it
wrap and grow multiline (`AcceptsReturn` / `TextWrapping = Wrap`, with a
`MinWidth` inside the left pane) rather than clip. Lowest priority.

---

## Part D — Sample geometry & R/T support

**D.0 Geometry semantics (operator).** A sample's `SubstrateKind`
(`ElementId.fs:80-82`) is `ThinFilm | Plate`. A **thin film** is 0+ layers on a
semi-infinite substrate and supports **reflectance only** (transmittance is
meaningless; the lower half-space is semi-infinite, `Propagation.sampleToSystem`
`Propagation.fs:249-256`). A **plate** is 0+ layers on a thick plate substrate,
which MUST be specified, and supports **reflectance and/or transmittance** with
the lower medium always vacuum.

**D.1 A Plate requires a substrate.** Extend `validateSample`
(`ElementId.fs:802-811`, today only blank-name and structurally-empty) to reject
a `Plate` whose `structure.substrate` is `None` with a typed `InvalidSample`.

**D.2 R/T support reuses `Emission`.** Give `Sample` (`ElementId.fs:178-188`) a
`supportedEmission : Emission` field reusing the existing DU, geometry-constrained
(operator, 010/Q3): a **thin film** carries `EmitReflectedOnly` (an invariant —
whenever the geometry is `ThinFilm`, `supportedEmission` MUST be
`EmitReflectedOnly`, and the R branch cannot be cleared); a **plate** defaults to
`EmitBoth` and MAY be constrained to R-only or T-only through the existing smart
setters `Emission.withReflected` / `withTransmitted` (`Placement.fs:126-138`),
whose never-neither rule then falls out for free. The seeds set it (every seeded
sample is `ThinFilm`, so `EmitReflectedOnly`). The sample editor exposes R / T
checkboxes driven by those setters (Plate only; a thin film shows R fixed on),
threaded through the editor `Model`, its dirty snapshot (`SampleEditSnapshot`,
`SampleEditorView.fs:110`), `toSample` (`:369-376`), and `init`.

**D.3 Supported emission bounds the placed element and the experiment.** A
placed sample's default `Placement.emission` derives from the sample's
`supportedEmission` rather than the generic `defaultEmission`
(`Placement.fs:168-171`), and the experiment's `MeasurementMode` defaults from it
through the existing `MeasurementMode.ofEmission` (`Experiments.fs:106-110`);
when the sample supports both, the experiment MAY still choose R, T, or both.

**D.4 Substrate "Set…" works for a film-less Plate (operator).** The substrate
"Set from chosen" button is enabled only by `hasChosenMaterial`
(`SampleEditorView.fs:961`, `:921-922`), and a chosen material only arises from
picking one for an existing film-layer row — so a `Plate` with no films cannot
set its substrate. Give the substrate "Set…" its own material pick that opens the
Materials window in Select state targeted at the substrate slot, reusing the
per-layer flow: add a `SampleSubstrateTarget` case to `SelectionTarget`
(`WindowMode.fs:31-33`) beside `SampleLayerTarget`, model the substrate "Set…"
on `chooseMaterialForLayer` (`SampleEditorView.fs:700-720`), and route the
targeted return to `SampleStackMsg.SetSubstrate` (`SampleStackEditor.fs:95`,
`:367`). The lower half-space stays vacuum.

